---
import Layout from "../../Base.astro";
import SimpleHero from "../../components/SimpleHero.astro";
import FeaturedArticles from "../../components/FeaturedArticles.astro";
import ServicesRelatedWork from "../../components/ServicesRelatedWork.astro";
import "../../styles/pages/_services-list.scss";
---

<Layout
  title="Website &amp; Marketing Copywriting | Kelp Agency"
  description="Clear website, ecommerce, and campaign copy that explains your offer, answers buyer questions, and supports the next step."
>
  <SimpleHero
    tagline="Marketing & Messaging Services"
    title="Copywriting"
    description="When your business knows what it does but customers still do not get it, the problem is usually the words. We write copy that makes the offer clear, builds trust, and gives people a reason to move."
    lottieFile="copywriting.json"
  />
  <div class="service-body">
    <div class="wrapper wrapper--narrow">
      <h2>Where teams get stuck</h2>
      <p>
        Many businesses know their offer inside and out, but that clarity never
        makes it onto the page. Website copy gets vague, ads sound
        interchangeable, and sales material asks the customer to do too much
        work.
      </p>
      <p>
        When the message is unclear, the audience leaves with questions instead
        of confidence.
      </p>
      <h2>How we fix it</h2>
      <p>
        We start by understanding the audience, the offer, and the context the
        copy needs to support. Then we turn subject-matter expertise into
        writing that is easier to follow and easier to act on.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Website pages and hero sections</li>
        <li>Product and service descriptions</li>
        <li>Case studies and about pages</li>
        <li>Blog Articles</li>
        <li>Ad headlines and campaign copy</li>
        <li>Social Media Posts</li>
        <li>Brochures, postcards, posters, and other marketing collateral</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For website projects, that usually means starting with a sitemap and
        content plan so we know what each page needs to say, in what order, and
        for whom. For campaigns or collateral, we focus on the goal of the piece
        and the response it needs to generate.
      </p>
      <h2>Research Before the First Headline</h2>
      <p>
        We gather input from the people closest to the customer: sales
        conversations, support questions, product details, existing content, and
        real examples of the work. Then we organize the message into pages and
        sections that match how people research a decision. That can include
        service pages, product descriptions, landing pages, email, campaign
        copy, and case studies.
      </p>
      <h2>Write for People and Discovery</h2>
      <p>
        Search and AI tools work best with specific, accurate information. We
        use descriptive headings, clear answers, and internal links while
        keeping the copy useful to a human reader. We do not promise that a
        phrase or schema field will force an AI recommendation. In <a href="/work/hsr/">HS&amp;R</a>,
        Kelp combined content strategy and copy with a HubSpot website migration
        and development project. Discuss the pages your customers need.
      </p>
      <h2>How This Works in Practice</h2>
      <p>
        The strongest copy usually comes from specifics that already exist
        inside the business: customer questions, sales objections, product
        details, service boundaries, and examples of work. We turn that
        information into a page hierarchy people can scan, then write the fuller
        explanations they need before acting. Search phrases are considered in
        that structure without making every paragraph sound like a keyword list.
      </p>
      <h2>What the Work Includes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Message and page-outline development</li>
        <li>Website and landing-page copy</li>
        <li>Product, campaign, and email copy where needed</li>
        <li>Editing for clarity, consistency, and search intent</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>What Moving Forward Looks Like</h2>
      <p>
        Start with a page or campaign that customers routinely misunderstand. We
        can use that work to establish a voice and page structure before
        extending it across the site.
      </p>
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <h3>Can AI write the first draft?</h3>
      <p>
        It can help explore options, but the published copy still needs verified
        details, real examples, editorial judgment, and a voice that belongs to
        your business.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/contact/" class="button button--green">
          Talk Through Your Messaging
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
    <ServicesRelatedWork tag={70} />
  </div>
  <FeaturedArticles />
</Layout>
